<%# ** "YOUR ACCOUNTS" — ONE CARD, ONE QUESTION PER ACCOUNT (account-openings spec §3). ** It was
    `_new_account.html.erb`, the add-account card, and it has absorbed the two onboarding steps that
    used to follow it: `_fund_account.html.erb` (a movement from main per account) and
    `_opening_balance.html.erb` (the one-time main correction) are DELETED. Henry, 2026-09-06:

      > "Not many people are going to know how much TOTAL money they have and then divide it all off.
      > Instead they just want to put in what their accounts are currently worth. The fact you have
      > to get the ordering exactly right is bad user experience."

    So the card is a LIST OF ROWS — every account that has not yet said what it holds, each with its
    own field and its own Save — and the order they are saved in changes nothing (`AccountOpening`
    makes every save self-contained). The rows disappear one by one; when the last one goes the card
    is the add row alone, and onboarding is over without anything having announced itself.

    ADDING AN ACCOUNT ASKS BOTH THINGS AT ONCE, because for the user they are one act: a name and
    what is in it. The balance is optional — "I don't know yet" is a real answer and leaves the
    account a row of its own above.

    Locals: `presenter` (HomePresenter), `bank_account` (an unsaved account-type Pool, or the
    rejected one with its errors when BankAccountsController re-renders this screen at 422),
    `balance` (the figure typed into the add row when that 422 was about the balance rather than the
    name). %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" aria-labelledby="your-accounts-heading"
         data-accounts-onboarding>
  <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200">
    <h3 id="your-accounts-heading" class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Your accounts</h3>
  </div>

  <%# THE ROWS, ONE PER UNANSWERED ACCOUNT. `#onboarding_accounts` is the one place the split between
      "still to answer" and "finished" is made, so an account cannot both hold a row here and render
      its card inside the accounts line below. %>
  <% presenter.onboarding_accounts.each do |account| %>
    <div class="border-b border-gray-200">
      <%= render "opening_row",
          account: account,
          name: account.name,
          url: bank_account_opening_path(account),
          method: :post,
          value: presenter.rejected_opening_for(account)&.typed,
          data: { account_row: account.name } %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= simple_form_for bank_account, as: :bank_account, url: bank_accounts_path do |f| %>
    <div class="px-6 py-4 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-start gap-3">
      <div class="flex-1">
        <%= f.input :name,
            label: "Account name",
            placeholder: "e.g., Ally Savings",
            hint: "Add every account your income reaches." %>
      </div>
      <div class="flex-1">
        <%# NO `min` ON THIS ONE, and it is the only field on the screen without one: the FIRST
            account a user adds becomes their main account, and an overdrawn checking account is a
            fact (spec §4). `AccountOpening` refuses a negative for anyone else. %>
        <%= f.input :balance,
            as: :decimal,
            label: "What's in it right now",
            placeholder: "0.00",
            required: false,
            input_html: { step: 0.01, value: balance },
            hint: "Optional — you can say later." %>
      </div>
      <div class="sm:pt-7">
        <%= f.button :submit, "Add an account", class: "btn btn-primary" %>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</section>
